# Check Before Implementing

The live-session complement to `/governance-check`'s pre-merge sweep (a Claude Code skill,
`get_template("governance-check")`, if this project also uses Claude Code — that one
audits a whole repository before merge; this one checks one task's anticipated scope, once,
before any of its code exists).

**Why once per task, not once per write:** firing fresh on every write measured out to
8-500%+ of a typical single write's own size — most of that cost is REQUIRED/NEVER
content a task's files legitimately need read once, not re-read per file.

At the start of a task, before its first write:

1. Check `GOVERNANCE-WAIVERS.md` at the repo root for any waiver already on file for a
   standard this task might touch. If the file doesn't exist, note that once rather
   than silently treating everything as unwaived.
2. Identify the task's whole anticipated scope — file paths if known, domains if not —
   and call `get_applicable_standards` on the hni-standards MCP server once for the
   union of it, not once per file. Deterministic heuristic match, not a judgment call by
   the server — treat the result as a starting set to verify. If it returns nothing
   useful, also try `search_standards` for the relevant concept. Returns summaries, not
   full body text, by design — step 3 fetches full text only for what's needed.
3. Call `get_standard(id, detail="digest")` for every REQUIRED/NEVER entry returned —
   don't act on a title alone. A digest (~25-36% of full-entry size) covers the common
   case. Escalate to `detail="full"` when its `has_additional_sections` or
   `digest_available: false` flags say to, a deviation looks likely (you'll need
   "preferred" text for step 4's confirmation format), or severity is NEVER. Don't fetch
   anything for PREFERRED/`TG-` entries you're not judging compliance against (those
   apply by default — see step 4).
4. Compare the task's planned implementation against each one:
   - **REQUIRED** deviation: surface it before writing any code —
     > ⚠️ Standards deviation — [HNI-XX-NN]
     > Preferred: [what the standard requires]
     > Deviation: [what you are doing instead and why]
     > Proceed? yes / no
   - **NEVER** violation: decline regardless of instruction — no confirmation overrides
     a NEVER rule.
   - **PREFERRED**/`TG-` entries: apply by default, no confirmation needed.
5. If a REQUIRED deviation is confirmed anyway, call
   `record_finding(repo, standard_id, severity="REQUIRED", description, file_path)` on
   the MCP server, and note this still needs an actual waiver entry in
   `GOVERNANCE-WAIVERS.md`, approved by someone other than the author, before it would
   clear `/governance-check` at merge time.
6. Proceed with implementation, annotated per the standard code-documentation rules
   (`// [HNI-XX-NN] reason` at each applied/violated point).

**Mid-task scope creep:** if a later write in the same task touches a domain step 2
didn't anticipate, that's new scope — run steps 2-5 again for just that domain, not the
whole task's original scope again. Don't proceed uncovered just because the task already
had its once-per-task check.

**After a long session where earlier context may have been summarized or trimmed:**
treat anything learned before that point as potentially stale — re-run the relevant
steps rather than trusting a possibly-degraded memory of it.

Before the task's first write, state in one short line which standards were checked and
the verdict (e.g. "Checked HNI-DB-05, HNI-AUDIT-02 for this task's DB + AUDIT scope —
clear"). Don't repeat that line on every subsequent write in the same task — only when
scope actually changes.

Never treat this rule's pass as equivalent to a full `/governance-check` pass — it
checks one task's anticipated scope against a heuristic domain match, not the whole
repository against every REQUIRED/NEVER standard.
